<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicolas Grunitzky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1913–1969</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicolas Grunitzky was a Togolese politician who led parties favouring close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in French Togoland and became prime minister of the autonomous republic in 1956. Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylvanus-olympio">Sylvanus Olympio</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-togolese-unity">Committee of Togolese Unity</a> in the UN-supervised elections of 1958, he returned after Olympio's assassination in 1963 as president, until Gnassingbé Eyadéma overthrew him in 1967.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicolas-grunitzky.jpg" alt="Nicolas Grunitzky" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moshe Pridan (Israel National Photo Collection) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_Grunitzky_1964.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
